# =============================================================================
# THE REFERENCE KIT ONTOLOGY  —  copy this file, rename it, fill it in.
# =============================================================================
#
#   TEMPLATE VERSION: 1.0.0        (see "TEMPLATE CHANGELOG" at the bottom)
#   CURRENT FOR:      kit-base 0.11.0
#   MAINTAINED BY:    the guardian seat (tr1p-l3x). Changes here are ontology
#                     direction and route through Rob like any other.
#
# This file is a WORKING ONTOLOGY, not a snippet. It parses, and it is meant to
# stay parsing — if you change it, run a Turtle parser over it before you push.
# That is the whole reason it is a .ttl and not a markdown code block: a
# template that cannot be validated rots without telling anyone.
#
# It describes a fictional "garden" kit. Every construct a real kit needs is
# here exactly once, in the order you should write them.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO KINDS OF COMMENT, AND DO NOT MIX THEM UP
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   `#` comments  — teaching. For whoever reads or edits this ontology. Write
#                   as much as the decision deserves. None of it ships anywhere.
#
#   rdfs:comment  — AUTHORING INSTRUCTION. `git lex create` lifts this VERBATIM
#                   into the document beside the key, so it is read by an author
#                   at the moment of typing. ONE SHORT LINE. Imperative. Name the
#                   tempting wrong action.
#
# The rdfs:comment values below are deliberately terse. That is not laziness —
# it is the standard being demonstrated. The long explanations all live in `#`.
#
# Why this matters, with a receipt: copia once had 15 multi-valued properties
# whose comment read "Repeat for each." Cardinality was right, every reader
# behaved, and the SENTENCE was wrong — an author hears "repeat" as "write the
# key again," a repeated YAML key is undefined, and our parser takes last-wins.
# Cost: 28 documents, ~60 values, one Outfit listing 8 items reduced to 1. The
# clause that fixed it was "do not repeat the key" — naming the wrong action,
# because "multi-valued" alone had not stopped it.
# =============================================================================

@prefix garden:  <https://repolex.ai/ontology/garden/> .
@prefix git-lex: <https://repolex.ai/ontology/git-lex/> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. THE ONTOLOGY HEADER
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The IRI has NO trailing slash here; the @prefix above does. That is correct
# and not a typo — one names the ontology, the other is the namespace base.
#
# Bump owl:versionInfo on every change. It is how a reader knows whether the
# installed copy matches what you are looking at.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

<https://repolex.ai/ontology/garden> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Garden Kit Ontology" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this kit is for, in one sentence." ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.1.0" .

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. THE CHANGELOG BLOCK  —  write this EVERY time, directly under the header
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Newest first. One block per version. This is the primary source for what the
# contract IS — more authoritative than the data, and the place to look before
# grepping a corpus to find out how something is supposed to work.
#
# Each block says: what changed, WHY, who ruled it, and what was deliberately
# NOT done. The refusals are the most valuable part six months later, because
# they are what stop the same proposal coming back.
#
# v0.1 (YYYY-MM-DD, Rob-ruled): FIRST CUT.
#   (1) + garden:Plant, foldered, with garden:plantId as its anchor.
#   (2) + garden:health as a closed enum — three values, because a free string
#   would have made "which plants are struggling" a text search.
#   DELIBERATELY NOT ADDED: garden:notes. It has no reader, so it fails the
#   why-test and belongs in the document body.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. A FOLDERED CLASS  —  a document type someone writes by hand
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `git-lex:foldered true` means: scaffold a folder, generate a __Plant.md
# template, add `git lex create plant`. Leave it off and the class is graph-only
# (see §8).
#
# git-lex:authoringGuidance says what belongs in the BODY. It is delivered by
# `create` and written into the __Plant.md template; it does NOT land in the
# document, and it is NEVER enforced — no gate, no warning, nothing in verify.
# Headings with one line each. If you are writing paragraphs you are writing a
# manual, and a manual belongs somewhere this can point at.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

garden:Plant a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf git-lex:Thing ;
    git-lex:foldered true ;
    rdfs:label "Plant" ;
    rdfs:comment "A plant under care: what it is, where it lives, how it is doing." ;
    git-lex:authoringGuidance """
## Condition
What it looks like right now. Observations, not plans.

## History
What you have done to it, and when.
""" .

# `rdfs:subClassOf git-lex:Thing` is REQUIRED, and it is the line people forget.
# Without it the class is not a Thing, so none of the eight universal properties
# (id, title, description, abstract, cue, relatedToId, dateCreated, dateUpdated)
# apply to it.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. IDENTITY  —  every foldered class needs exactly one strategy. THERE ARE TWO.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pick one deliberately and say which in a comment. Neither is deprecated.
#
#   (a) CONVENTION ANCHOR — a <class>Id property, shown below. The older and
#       still the common case across soul, squad, and most of copia.
#
#   (b) THE UNIVERSAL git-lex:id — no <class>Id at all. Rob-ruled 2026-08-23 for
#       copia's newest classes: the Thing plane already supplies an authored,
#       rename-surviving id, so a <class>Id would be a SECOND identity for the
#       same node. `title` carries what a per-class name field would have.
#
# If you choose (b), WRITE A COMMENT SAYING SO. Otherwise the next reader — and
# every linter — reads a missing <class>Id as an oversight rather than a choice.
#
# The rest of this section is (a).
#
# Named <lowerCamelClassName> + "Id". Derived by convention, NOT configurable —
# there is no annotation pointing elsewhere, on purpose.
#
# THIS IS THE KEYSTONE. It is what lifts the document's facts onto the Thing
# plane. Without it, every kit-dotted key silently falls back to the File node
# and dies the next time the file moves.
#
# Two consequences worth knowing before you edit one:
#   - NEVER inherit identity from an abstract parent. The tooling derives the
#     property from the CONCRETE class's own name, so an inherited id is
#     invisible to it. copia's Set/Sequence once inherited groupId and every
#     Set anchored to nothing.
#   - Identity properties are the ONE exception to "delete unused properties
#     freely." Deleting this line demotes every existing Plant to the File
#     plane, silently. If you must, the reader's fallback lane goes in the same
#     change.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

garden:plantId a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "plantId" ;
    rdfs:comment "Unique identifier for this plant — same as the filename." ;
    rdfs:domain garden:Plant ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. A PLAIN PROPERTY
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Before you add ANY property, run the why-test:
#
#     Name the system that breaks without it. Cannot name one? It goes in the
#     document body, where grep finds it fine.
#
# Exactly four shapes reliably pass, because only these have a reader: an id, a
# strict enum, a required field, a real edge. "It would be nice to query" is not
# a system. Popularity is not either — `tags` had 699 uses across 12 repos and
# was refused, because no application read it.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

garden:wateredOn a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "wateredOn" ;
    rdfs:comment "The date this plant was last watered, YYYY-MM-DD." ;
    rdfs:domain garden:Plant ;
    rdfs:range xsd:date .

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. AN ENUM  —  declare the value set as a datatype, then point a range at it
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Any other value is rejected at save with a clear message. That rejection IS
# the justification — an enum passes the why-test because something enforces it.
#
# Put the allowed values in the rdfs:comment too. The author is choosing one at
# the moment they read it, and making them open the ontology to find the list is
# a small cruelty repeated forever.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

garden:HealthValue a rdfs:Datatype ;
    owl:oneOf ( "thriving" "okay" "struggling" ) .

garden:health a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "health" ;
    rdfs:comment "One of: thriving, okay, struggling." ;
    rdfs:domain garden:Plant ;
    rdfs:range garden:HealthValue .

# For a bound rather than a list, declare the base type and its restrictions:
#
#   garden:RatingValue a rdfs:Datatype ;
#       owl:onDatatype xsd:integer ;
#       owl:withRestrictions ( [ xsd:minInclusive 1 ] [ xsd:maxInclusive 5 ] ) .
#
# Translated facets: minInclusive, maxInclusive, minExclusive, maxExclusive,
# minLength, maxLength, pattern. Anything else warns that the bound is NOT
# enforced — declared bounds are never silently dropped.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. REFERENCE PROPERTIES  —  READ THIS TWICE, IT IS THE SHARPEST TRAP
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# rdfs:range is not documentation here. It is a CONTRACT ABOUT WHAT THE AUTHOR
# TYPES, and git-lex parses the value differently depending on which you chose.
# Selection is EXACT EQUALITY on the range IRI — no subclass walk, and the
# property's NAME is never consulted.
#
#   LANE A   rdfs:range git-lex:Thing      →  <namespace/Class/id>   (brackets)
#   LANE B   rdfs:range <a concrete class> →  bare-id                (no brackets)
#
# THE TRAP: a bracketed value under a CONCRETE range does not error. It
# percent-encodes into the identifier —
#
#     you meant   →   https://repolex.ai/garden/Bed/south-wall
#     you got     →   https://repolex.ai/garden/Bed/%3Cgarden/Bed/south-wall%3E
#
# No warning. The reference points at an address nothing describes, and every
# join through it silently returns nothing.
#
# So: NAMING THE EXACT CLASS FEELS MORE CORRECT AND IS THE RISKIER CHOICE —
# not because Lane B is wrong, but because it commits your authors to the bare
# form, and a bracketed value typed into it fails silently instead of loudly.
#
# WHICH TO PICK:
#   Lane A when the target could be ANYTHING — any class, any kit. relatedToId
#     itself is the type case.
#   Lane B when the target is always ONE known class AND you want a dangling
#     reference to fail AT SAVE. The declared range is what makes that check
#     possible: git-lex derives the full IRI from range + bare id
#     (nquad.rs thing_iri_from_range), so a nonexistent target is caught.
#
# OPEN, AND ROB'S: typed relatedTo<Class>Id properties currently differ between
# kits. soul:relatedToPursuitId is Lane A (brackets); copia's
# relatedToScenarioId / relatedToPlaceId / relatedToBeingId are Lane B (bare,
# Rob-ruled 2026-08-23). If you are adding one, ASK before you declare it.
# The mechanism is not in dispute — only which lane the typed family should use.
#
# And note the range does ontological work either way. In RDFS a range is an
# INFERENCE RULE, not a filter:  P range C  +  x P y  ⊨  y a C. Nothing
# validates; the type is inferred. So the question is rarely "should we declare
# this?" and usually "what does the data already say, and is our ontology just
# declining to write it down?"
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Lane A — points anywhere, across any kit, no coordination needed.
garden:relatedToBedId a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "relatedToBedId" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Bed this plant relates to, written <garden/Bed/identifier>." ;
    rdfs:domain garden:Plant ;
    rdfs:range git-lex:Thing .

# Lane B — one known class in this kit, terse bare-id value.
garden:livesInBedId a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "livesInBedId" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Bed this plant is planted in — the bed's bare bedId." ;
    rdfs:domain garden:Plant ;
    rdfs:range garden:Bed .

# A multi-valued property. Note the clause doing the work: "do not repeat the
# key." That is the sentence that stopped 60 values from disappearing.
garden:companionPlantId a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "companionPlantId" ;
    rdfs:comment "Multi-valued — one <garden/Plant/identifier> per list item; do not repeat the key." ;
    rdfs:domain garden:Plant ;
    rdfs:range git-lex:Thing .

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. A GRAPH-ONLY CLASS  —  real, but nobody authors a document for it
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No git-lex:foldered, so no folder, no template, no create command. Something
# else builds these — a tool, an importer, a derivation.
#
# It is STILL a Thing if it has its own identity. "Foldered" and "is a Thing"
# are different questions; they used to coincide, which is how an earlier
# definition of Thing came to say "every foldered class is a Thing." That was
# true of what happened to exist at the time and was never the definition.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

garden:Bed a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf git-lex:Thing ;
    rdfs:label "Bed" ;
    rdfs:comment "A planting bed. Derived from the site survey; not hand-authored." .

garden:bedId a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "bedId" ;
    rdfs:comment "Unique identifier for this bed." ;
    rdfs:domain garden:Bed ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. CARDINALITY  —  say it once, at the bottom, for the whole class
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Single-valued properties get owl:maxCardinality 1. A REQUIRED property gets
# owl:minCardinality 1 — but think hard before you add one to an existing class:
# a new requirement walls people out of their own repos on their next save.
#
# The safe sequence is DECLARE TOOTHLESS → BACKFILL → REQUIRE, and the requiring
# is a separate decision made later, not a thing you slip in with the field.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

garden:Plant rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty garden:plantId     ; owl:minCardinality 1 ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty garden:plantId     ; owl:maxCardinality 1 ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty garden:health      ; owl:maxCardinality 1 ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty garden:livesInBedId ; owl:maxCardinality 1 ] .

# =============================================================================
# BEFORE YOU PUBLISH  —  the checklist
# =============================================================================
#   [ ] Rob has ruled on it. Ontology direction is not a squaddie request.
#   [ ] Every property passes the why-test — you can NAME the reader.
#   [ ] Every foldered class has rdfs:subClassOf git-lex:Thing.
#   [ ] Every foldered class has its <class>Id, on the concrete class.
#   [ ] Every class has rdfs:label AND rdfs:comment.
#   [ ] Every property has rdfs:domain, rdfs:range, rdfs:label, rdfs:comment.
#   [ ] Every rdfs:comment is ONE line of instruction, naming the wrong action.
#   [ ] Every enum lists its values in the comment.
#   [ ] Reference properties declare the lane you actually want (§7).
#   [ ] You deleted unused properties rather than deprecating them — EXCEPT
#       identity properties, which are read (§4).
#   [ ] If the meaning of existing values changed, the READER SHIPPED FIRST.
#   [ ] owl:versionInfo bumped, changelog block written (§2).
#   [ ] IT PARSES. Actually run a parser. Do not assume.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# TEMPLATE CHANGELOG  —  this file's own history, newest first
# =============================================================================
# 1.0.0 (2026-08-25, tr1p-l3x, Rob-requested): First cut. Written because th34
#   and Rob needed something to point at while authoring new classes, and
#   because "the proper way to write one" existed only across four docs, several
#   changelogs, and one seat's head.
#   Shipped as a PARSING .ttl rather than a markdown template, deliberately: a
#   template that cannot be validated rots silently, and this one is meant to
#   become a pre-publish check. The checklist above is written as mechanical
#   assertions on purpose — every line is something a linter can test.
# =============================================================================
